From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 20:03:07 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07/18] fix(refinery): scope the merge-approval gate's PR
 lookup to origin (gcp-3ty)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The gate read the WRONG REPOSITORY. `lookup_pr()` called `gh pr view <n>` with
no `--repo`, and gh resolves its base repo with its own heuristic, which on a
fork that also carries an `upstream` remote answers the PARENT. This rig is
exactly that shape (origin Hough-labs, upstream gastownhall), so a gate asked
about PR 2 of the fork was handed PR 2 of the parent — an unrelated Discord
change, long since merged — and refused with `pr_not_open state=MERGED`. Every
approval was refused, so nothing could ever land with require_merge_approval
on: a permanent merge deadlock that reads as reviewer inaction. It is an
integrity defect as much as an availability one; a verdict derived from a
repository with no relation to the change under review is not a gate, and its
fail-closed outcome was accidental rather than designed.

The script already knew how to do this correctly — `lookup_pr_rest()` parsed
`git remote get-url origin` and hit the origin-scoped REST endpoint — so the
two lookup paths disagreed, and because `command -v gh` succeeds here the wrong
one always won. The fix lifts that parsing into a single `resolve_origin_repo`
helper (delimited with the same `origin-repo-resolution` sentinels the mr lane
uses), has both paths consume it, and passes `--repo "$ORIGIN_REPO"` to
`gh pr view`. Resolution now runs once, before either path, so the gh and REST
answers cannot diverge again. An unresolvable origin is `undecidable` (exit 2),
never a fallback to gh's guess — an unscoped lookup is the bug, so there is no
safe degraded mode; this follows mol-refinery-patrol's `lookup_pr_info()`
precedent from gcp-6qp, which made the same call one layer up and missed this
script. `ssh://git@github.com/` joins the recognized remote forms so a rig with
that remote is not pushed from working-by-accident into undecidable.

Validated three ways. The new regression test drives a gh stub that returns
DIFFERENT pr state/head depending on whether `--repo` was passed — a stub that
ignored the flag could not observe this bug — and asserts the gate reads the
origin-scoped answer over the upstream one, for both https and ssh remotes;
against the pre-fix gate it fails with the exact production symptom
(`refused reason=pr_not_open pr=7 state=MERGED`). A second test pins the
fail-closed half: an unreadable or unsupported origin is undecidable and gh is
never invoked at all. Gate tests also gained a `git` stub so they no longer
read the ambient checkout's remote. Live: the standing gcp-a22 approval at
6686f3c9d68bc4f5192767e7eff6da7c0e6d1f7e is refused (exit 1) by the pre-fix
gate and approved (exit 0) by this one. All five gastown/tests/test_*.sh pass;
shellcheck is clean on both files.

Landing note: per the ruling on this bead, this branch stops at the PR. The
bug locks out its own repair, so the mayor hand-merges this one commit with the
gate left on; do not disable the gate or edit the pack cache to unblock it.
